is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswb1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF on the distribution switches’ management SVI (br0.99). Mechanism: dswa1 and dswb1 are sending OSPF hellos on VLAN 99 with a /32 netmask, while their neighbors expect /28. Those hellos are rejected as “NetworkMask mismatch,” causing repeated OSPF control-plane churn and occasional brief stalls seen campus-wide.

Localization: 
- dswa1 br0.99 has two IPs: 10.0.0.2/28 and 10.0.0.1/32 (ip -br addr show br0.99).
- dswb1 br0.99 has 10.0.0.18/28 and 10.0.0.17/32.
- Neighbors (dswa2, dswb2) continuously log OSPF errors: “NetworkMask mismatch on br0.99 … configured prefix length is 28, but hello packet indicates 32” (FRR logs on dswa2, dswb2). Despite adjacencies eventually reaching Full, these repeated mismatched hellos force extra OSPF processing and LSA retransmissions.

Root cause: Secondary /32 addresses were mistakenly configured on the OSPF-active management interface (br0.99) of dswa1 and dswb1, causing OSPF hellos to advertise a /32 mask on a /28 segment.

Corrective fix:
- Remove the /32 secondaries from br0.99 on dswa1 (10.0.0.1/32) and dswb1 (10.0.0.17/32).
- If those /32s are needed as stable identifiers, move them to loopback (lo) and keep br0.99 only with the /28 address. Ensure OSPF runs on br0.99 with a consistent /28 mask, and advertise loopbacks as passive.
- Alternatively, make br0.99 passive in OSPF if adjacency over that VLAN isn’t required.

This will stop the OSPF hello mask mismatches, reduce control-plane churn, and eliminate the periodic network “freezes.” === END ===